but I looked into it, do some research.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:02.990
And then I realized that
they were a part of the best

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:06.830
cybersecurity leader in
industry, in the whole country.

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:09.410
And I said, okay, I'm
gonna give it a chance,

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:12.400
so although I didn't have
experience in higher education,

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:15.300
at that time I had never
worked as a student advisor

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:18.010
in the higher education
setting or for a college,

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:18.843
I applied.

00:11:18.843 --> 00:11:21.150
Anyway, I stepped out on
faith out better on myself,

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.310
and I said, okay, I'm
gonna take this step,

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:26.048
I'm gonna bet on myself
and I'm gonna apply.

00:11:26.048 --> 00:11:26.881
And that's just what I did,

00:11:26.881 --> 00:11:27.750
I was afraid,

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.361
I was scared, (chuckle),

00:11:29.361 --> 00:11:31.470
I was nervous, but I got a call back.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.200
And several interviews later,

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:36.700
I landed the job and we're
not going on two years

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:37.533
in December,

00:11:37.533 --> 00:11:38.950
and I haven't looked back since,

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:40.920
and I'm so thankful for the opportunity,

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.550
and I've learned so much
about cybersecurity.

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:44.960
It is so interesting,

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:49.420
and I'm just excited to teach
and inspire those out there

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.510
who are watching this
to want to defend again,

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:56.460
like our cyber war, because
cybersecurity is going to be...

00:11:56.460 --> 00:12:01.140
and is a huge and critical
piece of our infrastructure,

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:02.400
in this country.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.129
And it's something that
we're gonna need more people,

00:12:05.129 --> 00:12:08.630
regardless of what major or
career path you come from,

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.630
regardless of your background,

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:11.930
you don't have to fit a mold,

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:15.110
there's no prototype of
what you need to look like,

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:20.040
or be, or like, or do
to join cybersecurity.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.960
We want people from all walks of life,

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.580
whether you're into art and
you're really good at it,

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.820
or you're really good
at software engineering,

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:30.291
we need all of you from all
walks of life to come into the

00:12:30.291 --> 00:12:31.124
world of cybersecurity,

00:12:31.124 --> 00:12:33.840
to bring your diverse
perspectives and to help us

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.273
save the world.

00:12:35.273 --> 00:12:36.144
(laughs)

00:12:36.144 --> 00:12:36.977
- Yeah, I love it.

00:12:36.977 --> 00:12:39.280
Very few jobs we can credibly
talk about saving the world,

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:41.560
and one, I was very entertained

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:45.170
by the notion that you thought
the cybersecurity college

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:48.233
might itself be some
type of efficient attack.

00:12:48.233 --> 00:12:49.380
(laughing)

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:50.510
You might've,

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:54.496
but also I hear it in
your voice, the joy of ...

00:12:54.496 --> 00:12:55.810
and this is I think education generally,

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.240
but being an advisor because
you get a multiplier effect

00:12:58.240 --> 00:12:59.608
on your job,

00:12:59.608 --> 00:13:01.550
you're helping other people
discover their journeys,

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:04.450
and then they will then
help save the planet.

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:05.283
Hopefully.

00:13:05.283 --> 00:13:06.116
- Yes.

00:13:06.116 --> 00:13:08.760
- Madison, what about on your side?

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.330
And I'll give a slightly
different flavor to the question,

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:12.847
which is, it's one thing
for you early on to say,

00:13:12.847 --> 00:13:15.810
"Hey, you know, I like art
more than I like healthcare."

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.150
But there's another
thing where the education

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.470
and what you do in school
might be pretty different,

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:22.080
than what you end up doing for a job.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.320
I've seen people who go
to school for one thing,

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:26.331
but then when they get
the job, they're like,

00:13:26.331 --> 00:13:28.837
"Oh, this is what I have to
do on a day-to-day basis."

00:13:29.731 --> 00:13:31.650
What was the transition from school,

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:33.700
which it sound like you
really enjoyed art school,

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:35.640
or the design aspects of it to work,

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:36.930
was it, as you were expecting,

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:37.940
was it better than expected?

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:39.242
Was it worse than expected?

00:13:39.242 --> 00:13:40.383
How did you navigate that?

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.730
- I think this is a, a very,
very personal question,

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:48.550
to me, it was nothing like
my undergrad experience,

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:52.110
I can say, my internship
experiences and undergrad

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:55.490
reflect me being interested
in so many different things.

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:58.830
My freshman year I stayed
as a campus mentor,

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:01.170
on Howard's campus, just giving
tours, talking to people,

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:05.290
answering the same questions
that I asked as a senior,

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:06.210
being a freshman, I got it.

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:07.964
I got some answers, (chuckle).

00:14:07.964 --> 00:14:10.750
So I was able to relay
that my sophomore year,

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:13.830
I was a accounting intern at,

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:18.830
NTTs government military
headquarters in Virginia,

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.650
which is totally outside of design.

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:24.400
And I can go on about that a days,

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:27.200
but my junior year,

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:30.900
I was able to intern with
New York fashion week,

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:32.470
which was something
I've never done before,

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:34.860
I ended up in New York for a weekend,

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:38.450
really just running around
and experiencing that,

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:41.590
and my senior year is when
I actually landed my dream

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:44.840
internship with NTT in
El Segundo, California.

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:47.080
I'm essentially working in
their entertainment sector,

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:50.423
which is right on the merge
of their merger with a timer.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:55.180
And it was the craziest
experience (chuckle) ever.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:56.993
And after college, I actually,

00:14:58.130 --> 00:14:59.850
I received an offer from NTT,

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:02.150
but it wasn't necessarily
what I wanted to do,

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:06.150
more on the side of not in the location

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:07.740
where I wanted to in Texas,

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:08.960
I didn't really know anyone.

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:13.580
So I made the very unpopular
decision of declining that

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:17.730
offer, which is at the time I regretted,

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:19.860
all my friends were going
to these major places,

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:21.320
and that was still kind of home,

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.600
so I was like, ah, what's going on?

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:24.433
(chuckle)

00:15:24.433 --> 00:15:28.750
That's when I decided to start
small with IG building up,

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:30.940
you know, some clients I had in college,

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:32.330
as well as some local businesses,

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:34.830
and really just honing
in on my craft a bit more

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:37.750
while working kind of a ...

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:39.140
there's a term they use in LA,

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:41.040
when actors are waiting
for their big break,

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:43.300
but they're kinda just in-transit,

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:46.170
I had an in-transit job
and didn't really like it,

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:48.040
but I knew design was what I wanted to do,

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:49.310
so I kept working,

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:52.880
I actually ended up interning
last year with Pentagram,

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:54.740
for those who are familiar
with the design in the world,

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.180
it's actually the largest
independently design firm

00:15:57.180 --> 00:15:58.070
in the world.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:00.040
I worked under Elle
Parker and Michael Bierut,

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:04.450
and busted my butt for
about a year, (laughs),

00:16:05.426 --> 00:16:07.430
learning more and growing
and understanding.

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:10.320
And some nights (indistinct)
just gonna test,

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.000
I stayed up until like two
o'clock in the morning,

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.770
learning things and trying to get better,

00:16:14.770 --> 00:16:18.550
and a couple of months ago,
I accepted this job with CNN,

00:16:18.550 --> 00:16:19.560
which is my dream job.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:22.470
So my path is definitely a winding road,

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.380
and not a straight path whatsoever.

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:27.470
So to answer your question,
no, it was nothing,

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:30.823
like what I expected or what I imagined.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:33.860
- You know, there's this theme
that I'm getting from all

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:36.310
of y'all and this is true
in my journey as well that,

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:39.090
there's a lot of things
that maybe your college,

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:40.790
and your major might've helped you with,

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:42.040
but at the end of the day,

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:44.350
a lot of it is in each of your journeys,

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:45.440
you're asking questions,

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:46.940
you're constantly learning,

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:48.660
doing more and more things.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.750
There's a bunch of questions
I'm getting around grades

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:52.670
and test scores.

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:54.607
For example, Calvin Tran says,

00:16:54.607 --> 00:16:56.570
"Do grades determined if
you're going to be successful."

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:57.797
Alejandro Garcia,

00:16:57.797 --> 00:17:00.100
"Can your scores on the
SAT or ACT determined,

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:02.160
which careers may be good for you?"

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:03.510
Let's see, there's another question.

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:05.350
Lower, I think someone was asking,

00:17:05.350 --> 00:17:06.650
I think they've been discouraged,

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:08.720
for going into certain fields
maybe because they don't feel

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:10.790
I have the grades or the test scores.

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:15.380
What advice do you have
for students who are ...

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:16.550
because obviously when
you're in high school

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:17.860
and a lot of college,

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:20.440
your grades and your test
scores seem to matter a lot,

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:21.627
how do y'all think about that?

00:17:21.627 --> 00:17:23.080
And what have you seen in your own lives

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:27.080
or people around you, maybe
I'll randomly select Ernest,

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:28.280
you go first, (chuckle).

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:31.380
- Yeah, (clearing throat).

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:35.420
I came from a family where
education was the foundation

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:36.530
of everything.

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:38.500
Like we couldn't do ...

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:40.417
my sister and I did theater growing up,

00:17:40.417 --> 00:17:42.690
we were in karate with different sports.

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:44.370
We couldn't do extracurriculars,

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:47.780
if we weren't tight on our grades,

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:49.410
and our parents have your
praises all the time,

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:52.140
it'd be got honors roll,
honor roll or high honors.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:55.760
And sending that foundation
early in our lives really put us

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:59.660
on a great academic path all
the way up into college, right?

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:04.660
We were always striving to be
in honor classes, AP classes,

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:07.390
and then in college
and the honors program,

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:09.090
and Dean's list and all that.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:13.390
So I will always say it is
important to have a strong

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:18.120
educational basis because that
actually helps you also have

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:21.530
the right discipline with
everything else that you do

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:22.363
in life.

00:18:22.363 --> 00:18:24.360
Just because you're
working at a job at Google,

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:25.774
as a software engineer,

00:18:25.774 --> 00:18:27.430
and you're not getting
A's and B's and C's,

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:29.240
does not mean that
you're not (indistinct).

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:31.270
You're still gonna be graded, (chuckle),

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:33.530
on how well you're performing
at your job as well.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:36.840
So everything is always
associated with one another,

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.720
that being said, companies like Google,

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:41.850
don't ask you require you to
have a certain GPA to come

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:43.170
work or even apply.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:44.880
And I actually encourage all students,

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.351
no matter what you pay, they have,

00:18:47.351 --> 00:18:50.602
or what situation they're in
to always apply to all these

00:18:50.602 --> 00:18:51.435
companies,

00:18:51.435 --> 00:18:54.080
because we need to always have

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:55.750
people from a bunch of
different backgrounds,

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:58.973
with a bunch of different
thought processes, right?

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:03.740
But I still strongly recommend,

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:05.750
that you focus in on your grades,

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:08.509
get those good grades 'cause
good grades can lead to a lot

00:19:08.509 --> 00:19:09.342
of different things.

00:19:09.342 --> 00:19:11.010
So you're about to bring up earlier,

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:13.030
I'm a little worried about
talking about a little bit about

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:13.930
affording college,

00:19:13.930 --> 00:19:16.960
college is expensive,
what we talk about right.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:18.580
But the thing is,

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:20.700
especially with great
academics and make helps

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:21.880
get you scholarships,

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:23.820
so you can actually afford college.

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:26.780
The other thing also
with tech industry jobs,

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:28.810
if you guys don't know,
I mentioned earlier,

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:33.180
I internet Google and internship
is when you actually go

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:34.650
leaves, I mean you don't leave school,

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:35.640
but like during the summer,

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:36.890
when you're normally on summer break,

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:39.870
you're actually going to work
at these different companies.

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:42.320
And these internships are actually paid,

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:45.530
Google's internship alone on the summer,

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:49.440
you probably can make somewhere
between 12 to $15,000.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.750
That's a lot of money that can you,

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:55.083
you can take and put right
back into your education.

00:19:56.050 --> 00:20:00.180
So that's why I'm very big
on students making sure that

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:02.930
they're staying strong in their academics,

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:05.850
not so that they can get a 3.5
so they can get an internship

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:06.683
at Google.

00:20:06.683 --> 00:20:07.970
But so they have that discipline,

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:12.440
they have those foundations
in order to be successful,

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:15.750
to be able to get a job
or internship at Google.

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:19.860
So, then maybe in turn makes
college more affordable,

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:22.400
so basically the bottom
line of all this is it's all

00:20:22.400 --> 00:20:24.120
interdependent.

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:25.790
Make sure that you're
doing good in school,

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:27.980
'cause to promise you it's
gonna work out for you

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:29.500
in the end.

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:32.453
- Yeah, Delisha what are
your thoughts on grades?

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:35.130
- I'm gonna go on a little
different direction,

00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:38.910
but I will say that, I agree
that grades are important.

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:41.420
However, what I want
you all to understand,

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:42.753
and to hear me clearly.

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:47.163
Your SAT score is there any
test scores do not define you?

00:20:47.163 --> 00:20:51.260
Your GPA does not define
because we all know that,

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:52.170
everyone wants different,

00:20:52.170 --> 00:20:54.180
there's many situations
that may cause you to have

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:55.200
a low GPA.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:57.475
There are situations
that may cause you that

00:20:57.475 --> 00:21:00.098
to get the best score on the SAT or ACT,

00:21:00.098 --> 00:21:01.520
you may not be a great test taker.

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:03.720
I wasn't the best test taker,

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:06.290
but what I did do was try my best.

00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:08.650
And that's what I'm gonna
encourage you all to do,

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:11.730
is always ask yourself this question.

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:14.700
Am I trying my best number one,

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:19.700
number two, am I making sure
that this makes me happy?

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:25.160
And then number three,
am I betting on myself?

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:26.690
So those are the three
things that I want you to ask

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:28.790
yourself each time,

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:30.630
because at the end of the day,

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:32.720
especially in the world of cybersecurity,

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:34.490
we're not looking at your grades,

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:38.010
we're not looking at
your SAT or ACT scores.

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:42.560
What we're looking at is
how passionate are you?

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:45.810
How passionate are you
about wanting to rather save

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:47.590
the world in the cybersecurity space,

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:49.900
or save the world as software engineer,

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:51.910
or save the world by
creating beautiful art,

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:52.960
for the world to see.

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:57.230
The other thing that we're
looking at as well is,

00:21:57.230 --> 00:21:58.710
are you dedicated?

00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:01.310
Are you committed to being the best person

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:03.240
in the best version of yourself,

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:04.970
that you can be every single day?

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:09.970
Because again, it's really
important to not let any titles,

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:15.411
anybody, or anything defined
you because that's not,

00:22:15.411 --> 00:22:16.686
what's important in life,

00:22:16.686 --> 00:22:20.750
what's important in life,
is it making you happy?

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:22.270
Is it something you're passionate about?

00:22:22.270 --> 00:22:25.500
Is there a pathway that may
not be necessarily traditional

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:28.730
or linear that will get you
to where you need to go?

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:31.150
So that's kinda how I approach
and think about grading,

00:22:31.150 --> 00:22:33.500
'cause I don't want
students to get bogged down

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:36.963
and worry that, oh, if I have a 2.5,

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:39.350
that I'm not good enough.

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:40.500
Or if I have a three point,

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:42.760
oh, I'm not good enough
and I need to have it 4.0,

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:44.140
you don't have to be perfect,

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:45.630
we're not looking for perfect,

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:47.180
we're looking for unique,

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:48.280
we're looking for you.

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:50.970
- Yeah, I appreciate that.

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:52.970
And I love that framework
that you introduced.

00:22:52.970 --> 00:22:54.130
What about you Madison?

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:55.900
What are the roles of grades
have played with your,

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:57.930
and our test scores and how do you ...

00:22:57.930 --> 00:22:59.710
what advice do you have for folks,

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:01.060
to put that in perspective?

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:03.883
- Yeah.

00:23:03.883 --> 00:23:07.580
So I'm gonna just riffed
off a Delisha for a second.

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:11.460
